      <LanguageStandard>stdcpp20</LanguageStandard>
      <CallingConvention>StdCall</CallingConvention>
      <!--
        /EHsc, not /EHa. Under /EHa a catch(...) catches structured exceptions
        as well - access violations included - and this DLL is injected into
        Explorer, where continuing after one means continuing inside somebody
        else's corrupted process:

            Specifying /EHa and trying to handle all exceptions by using
            catch(...) can be dangerous. In most cases, asynchronous exceptions
            are unrecoverable and should be considered fatal. Catching them and
            proceeding can cause process corruption [...]

            we strongly recommend that you use ISO-standard C++ exception
            handling (/EHsc or /EHs)

          https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/build/reference/eh-exception-handling-model

        The deliberate __try/__except guards around foreign shell extension
        code are unaffected: "You can use structured exception handling syntax
        in the same module as code that's compiled by using /EHs." Only
        catch(...) stops swallowing faults it cannot do anything about.

        Set on every project rather than some, because the same page says to
        "never link object files compiled using /EHa to ones compiled using
        /EHs or /EHsc in the same executable module".
      -->
      <ExceptionHandling>Sync</ExceptionHandling>
      <!--
        Control Flow Guard requires compiler instrumentation as well as the
        existing /GUARD:CF linker option. A Guard-marked PE alone does not add
        checks to object files compiled without /guard:cf.

          https://learn.microsoft.com/cpp/build/reference/guard-enable-control-flow-guard
      -->
      <ControlFlowGuard>Guard</ControlFlowGuard>
